@database "sequential_thinking"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: sequential_thinking.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Sequential Thinking"
@{h1}Sequential Thinking

A scratchpad for step-by-step reasoning. Instead of thinking a hard problem
through in one answer, an agent writes it down one thought per tool call:
it numbers the steps, estimates how many it will need, goes back to correct
an earlier thought, and tries an alternative on a branch of its own. The
plugin keeps the chain and hands it back with every call; an optional hook
puts the latest thoughts in front of the model before each LLM call. The
chain lives in memory only -- it is working notes for a run, not a record.
It has no panel.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The thinking tool  " link tool}
  @{"  Revisions and branches  " link branches}
  @{"  Summary and clearing  " link summary}
  @{"  The session hook  " link hook}
  @{"  Where the state lives  " link state}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is on in the default configuration (@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}):

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    sequential_thinking:
      type: sequential_thinking
      enabled: true
      max_history_size: 100
      session_ttl_seconds: 3600
      enable_branching: true
      enable_revisions: true
      max_summary_thoughts: 10

      # Hook config for inject_active_sessions
      max_thoughts_in_prompt: 5
      show_branch_info: true
      format: markdown
@{body}

An agent sees the three tools when its allowlist names them:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+sequential_thinking/*"
@{body}

The hook is off until an agent switches it on; see
@{" The session hook " link hook}. No extra packages.

@{h2}Server settings

The settings stand directly in the server entry, as above -- not under a
@{tt}config:@{utt} block, where the plugin does not look. What is not set
takes the default below. They are read once, when the plugin starts.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
max_history_size | 100 | thoughts kept per session; older ones are dropped
session_ttl_seconds | 3600 | a session no thought was added to for this long is removed
enable_branching | true | false: every call with a branch_id is refused
enable_revisions | true | false: every revision is refused
max_summary_thoughts | 10 | thoughts in each answer's history, and the summary's default
max_thoughts_in_prompt | 5 | the hook: thoughts shown per session
max_sessions_in_prompt | 1 | the hook: sessions shown
show_branch_info | true | the hook: branch tags and the branch list
show_quick_actions | true | the hook: the parameter hints and examples
format | markdown | the hook: markdown, anything else is plain text
@{body}

The plugin stores nothing on disk; see
@{" Where the state lives " link state}.
@endnode

@node tool "The thinking tool"
@{h1}The thinking tool

The main tool is named like the plugin instance (@{tt}sequential_thinking@{utt}
in the default setup). Each call adds one thought to a session and returns
the session's latest thoughts.

@{h2}Which session a call writes to

@{bullet}With @{tt}session_id@{utt}: that session. An ID the plugin does
not know starts a new, empty session under that ID -- a typo starts a
second chain instead of failing.
@{bullet}Without it: the session this conversation used last. A
conversation with none gets a new session with a random 10-character ID,
returned in the answer.
@{bullet}A sub-agent runs in a conversation of its own, so it starts its
own session unless it is given an ID.

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Default in code | Meaning
thought | -- | the thought; empty or only blanks is refused
thought_number | 1 | the caller's step number; must be 1 or more, otherwise only echoed back
total_thoughts | 5 | the estimate of how many thoughts the problem needs; must be 1 or more
next_thought_needed | true | echoed back and shown in the status line; changes nothing
session_id | the conversation's last | see above
is_revision, revises_thought | false, -- | correct an earlier thought; see @{" Revisions and branches " link branches}
branch_from_thought, branch_id | -- | start or switch a branch; see @{" Revisions and branches " link branches}
needs_more_thoughts | false | true with a total_thoughts that did not grow adds a warning
idempotency_key | -- | a retry with the same key returns the first thought instead of adding one
@{body}

The camelCase spellings (@{tt}thoughtNumber@{utt}, @{tt}sessionId@{utt},
...) are accepted too. A number must be whole: 3, 3.0 and "3" are taken,
2.5 and true are refused. A flag takes true or false, also as text.
The length limits in the schema (10000 characters for a thought, 100 for a
branch name, 200 for a key) are not checked by the plugin.

@{h2}Numbering and the estimate

The plugin numbers the thoughts itself: a new thought gets the next number
of the session, whatever @{tt}thought_number@{utt} says; a revision keeps
the number of the thought it revises. The answer carries both,
@{tt}current_thought_number@{utt} (the plugin's) and
@{tt}client_thought_number@{utt} (the caller's).

Every call sets the estimate to @{tt}total_thoughts@{utt}. An estimate
below the number of thoughts so far is raised to that number, with a
warning. Revisions do not count: they repeat a step, they do not add one.

@{h2}Answers

@{table}
Field | Meaning
status | success or error
session_id | the session written to
event_id | the new entry's unique ID
current_thought_number, client_thought_number | see above
recorded_thoughts_count | thoughts the session holds now (at most max_history_size)
total_thoughts_estimate | the estimate after this call
next_thought_needed | as sent
progress | "Thought 7/12": the thoughts so far, revisions not counted, and the estimate
branch | the session's current branch
thought_history | the last max_summary_thoughts thoughts: event_id, number, content cut at 200 characters, branch, is_revision, revises_thought, timestamp
branch_summary | per branch: branched_from, thoughts_count, active, created_at, parent; left out when branching is off
warnings | only when there is one: the raised estimate, needs_more_thoughts without a larger estimate, memory use over 80 % of max_history_size
@{body}

A refused call answers @{tt}{"status": "error", "error": "..."}@{utt} and
changes nothing: it creates no session and does not keep one alive. The
texts:

@{table}
error | When
thought_number must be >= 1 | thought_number below 1
total_thoughts must be >= 1 | total_thoughts below 1
thought content cannot be empty | no text in thought
revises_thought is required when is_revision=true | a revision without a target
revises_thought must be >= 1 | a target below 1
Thought #N not found in session | a revision of a number the session does not hold
Revisions are disabled | enable_revisions is false
Branching is disabled | enable_branching is false and branch_id is set
Branch 'x' not found | a switch to a branch that does not exist
Thought #N not found | a new branch from a number the session does not hold
x must be a whole number, got ... | a number parameter that is not whole
x must be true or false, got ... | a flag that is neither
@{body}

Anything else that goes wrong answers the same way with the exception
text.

@{h2}Retries

A call with an @{tt}idempotency_key@{utt} the plugin has seen, whose
thought still exists, adds nothing. It answers with that thought's session,
number and event_id, an empty @{tt}thought_history@{utt} and the warning
"Idempotent request - returned cached result". The key is looked up in all
sessions of the process, not only this conversation's.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"thought": "The failing test only fails on Windows. Paths?", "thought_number": 1, "total_thoughts": 4, "next_thought_needed": true}
{"session_id": "k3x9q2m7ab", "thought": "The fixture joins with '/'.", "thought_number": 2, "total_thoughts": 4, "next_thought_needed": true}
{"session_id": "k3x9q2m7ab", "thought": "Not the fixture: pathlib is used there.", "thought_number": 2, "total_thoughts": 4, "next_thought_needed": true, "is_revision": true, "revises_thought": 2}
@{body}
@endnode

@node branches "Revisions and branches"
@{h1}Revisions and branches

@{h2}Revisions

@{tt}is_revision: true@{utt} with @{tt}revises_thought: N@{utt} writes a
new entry with number N; the old one stays. The target must be a number
the session still holds (after @{tt}max_history_size@{utt} dropped the
oldest, an old number is gone). The revision goes to the branch of the
latest version of thought N, not to the current branch, and carries the
earlier versions along: the summary shows their count as
@{tt}revision_count@{utt}.

@{h2}Branches

Every session starts on the branch @{tt}main@{utt}. The current branch is
kept between calls: new thoughts go there until the agent switches.

@{table}
Sent | Does
branch_from_thought and a new branch_id | creates the branch from thought N and makes it current; its parent is the branch thought N is on
branch_from_thought and an existing branch_id | switches to that branch, without a word
branch_id alone | switches; an unknown name is refused
branch_from_thought alone | ignored
@{body}

To go back, send @{tt}branch_id: "main"@{utt}. The numbering is shared by
all branches of a session: the thought after #4 on main is #5, on
whichever branch it lands. A branch is never closed; @{tt}active@{utt} is
always true.
@endnode

@node summary "Summary and clearing"
@{h1}Summary and clearing

@{h2}sequential_thinking_get_summary

The whole chain of one session, with the thoughts in full.

@{table}
Parameter | Default in code | Meaning
session_id | -- | required; missing: "session_id is required"
max_thoughts | max_summary_thoughts (10) | the latest thoughts to return; 0 or less returns all
include_branches | true | add the branch tree
@{body}

The answer: @{tt}status@{utt}, @{tt}session_id@{utt},
@{tt}total_thoughts@{utt} (thoughts held), @{tt}current_branch@{utt},
@{tt}thoughts@{utt} (event_id, number, content, timestamp, branch,
is_revision, revises_thought, revision_count), @{tt}created_at@{utt},
@{tt}last_accessed@{utt} and @{tt}branches@{utt}. An unknown ID answers
"Session X not found". Reading a summary does not keep a session alive;
only a call that adds a thought does.

@{h2}sequential_thinking_clear_history

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
session_id | the session to remove; unknown: "Session X not found"
@{body}

Without @{tt}session_id@{utt} it removes every session of the calling
conversation; called outside a conversation (directly, or from the
command line), only the sessions no conversation uses. The answer: @{tt}status@{utt}, @{tt}cleared_sessions@{utt}
and a @{tt}message@{utt} ("Cleared session k3x9q2m7ab (6 thoughts)").
@endnode

@node hook "The session hook"
@{h1}The session hook

@{tt}inject_active_sessions@{utt} runs before each LLM call and shows the
model where its reasoning stands, so it can go on without asking for a
summary. It is off until an agent switches it on.

@{h2}What it appends

A message at the end of the history, from the run (role
@{tt}developer@{utt}, marked with the instance name in @{tt}injected_by@{utt}, so two
instances do not replace each other's block):

@{bullet}While the conversation has no session: a short reminder that the
tool exists, with an example call and the parameter groups.
@{bullet}With one session: its ID, the progress (thoughts so far / estimate),
the last @{tt}max_thoughts_in_prompt@{utt} thoughts cut at 150 characters,
the branches, and the hints and examples.
@{bullet}With more (@{tt}max_sessions_in_prompt@{utt} above 1): the most
recently used first, the last 3 thoughts each (fewer when
max_thoughts_in_prompt is lower) cut at 100 characters.

@{code markdown}
## Active Sequential Thinking Session

**Session ID**: `k3x9q2m7ab`
**Progress**: 2/4 thoughts

**Recent thoughts:**
- **Thought #1**: The failing test only fails on Windows. Paths?
- **Thought #2**: The fixture joins with '/'.
- **Thought #2** (revises #2): Not the fixture: pathlib is used there.
@{body}

The message is added only when it differs from the last one the hook
added: a step that did not touch the tool adds nothing. When compaction
removed the last one, it comes back. Without messages or without a session
the hook does nothing; if it fails, the error is logged and the call goes
on without it. It has 5 seconds.

@{h2}Switching it on

In the agent's YAML; its settings go in the same entry:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  hooks:
    enabled: true
    overrides:
      sequential_thinking.inject_active_sessions:
        enabled: true
        max_thoughts_in_prompt: 3
        show_quick_actions: false
@{body}

@{h2}Settings

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
max_thoughts_in_prompt | 5 | thoughts per session; 0 or less: all, with several sessions 3
max_sessions_in_prompt | 1 | sessions shown, most recently used first
show_branch_info | true | the [branch] tags and the list of branches
show_quick_actions | true | the parameter hints and three example calls
format | markdown | anything else: plain lines with shorter thoughts and no hints
@{body}

A key in the agent's override wins; otherwise the key in the plugin's
server entry; otherwise the default above. The range in the schema (1 to
20, 1 to 5) is not enforced.
@endnode

@node state "Where the state lives"
@{h1}Where the state lives

Everything is held in the memory of the server process that runs the
plugin, and nothing else.

@{bullet}@{b}Per session@{ub}: a thinking session belongs to the
conversation (the chat session) that created or used it; the hook and a
call without @{tt}session_id@{utt} find it that way. A conversation can
have several.
@{bullet}@{b}Per process@{ub}: the store is shared by every agent and user
the process serves. The API and a command-line run are different processes
with different stores; a woken session runs in a new process and finds
nothing of the old one.
@{bullet}@{b}Restart@{ub}: nothing survives. A restart, a reload of the
plugin or a new process starts empty.

@{h2}How big it can grow

@{bullet}A session holds at most @{tt}max_history_size@{utt} (100)
thoughts; beyond it the oldest are dropped, and with them the numbers a
revision or a new branch could name.
@{bullet}Each thought keeps its text in full, and a revision the texts of
the versions before it.
@{bullet}There is no limit on the number of sessions. A session is removed
when no thought was added for @{tt}session_ttl_seconds@{utt} (one hour).
The check runs at the start of each call of the thinking tool -- with no
calls, an expired session stays in memory until the next one.
@{bullet}Idempotency keys and the conversation lookup are dropped with the
sessions and thoughts they point to.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

The tool descriptions come from the plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}. The
main tool tells the model that @{tt}total_thoughts@{utt} is a fixed upfront
estimate, not the current step. Refused calls answer with the error texts
in @{" The thinking tool " link tool}; the chat's status line shows
"Thought #3 added (3/4). Continue reasoning..." or "... ✓ Complete" when
@{tt}next_thought_needed@{utt} is false, and "Revising thought 2 with new
insights" or "Creating branch 'alt' from thought 2" while it works.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Every answer carries the last @{tt}max_summary_thoughts@{utt} thoughts
(200 characters each) and, with branching on, the branch tree -- so each
call repeats part of the chain. Tool answers are appended like any other;
nothing earlier changes. The hook appends its block at the end of the
history and only when it changed; it never rewrites the system prompt or
an earlier message, so the cached prompt in front of it stays intact.
Every changed block stays in the history, though: a long chain with the
hook on grows the conversation by one block per thought.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}A session ID is all it takes: any conversation that has one can
write to, read or clear that session, and an idempotency key matches a
thought of any conversation.
@{bullet}The server settings are read once; a change takes effect after a
restart.
@endnode
